# Regression test: found incidentally while building tests/pty_test_helpers.crush
# (see its own header comment), `source`-ing (or `execute::file`-ing) a file with 3 or
# more top-level statements, from within an already-running script, used to deadlock
# -- reproduced with plain `echo` statements, nothing pty-specific about it. Root
# cause: `execute::file`'s `source()` (in `src/lang/execute.rs`) sent *every*
# statement's value onto the same shared, bounded(1) output channel, with nothing
# ever draining between sends -- the first one or two fit in the buffer, a third
# blocked forever. Fixed by only ever forwarding the *last* statement's value (via
# `GlobalState::eval_statement`, the same "last statement, or discard" dispatch
# `Closure::eval_inner` and `JobListDefinition::eval` already use); every earlier
# statement now gets `black_hole()`, which `Job::eval` joins and discards on its own.
# tests/pty_test_helpers.crush works around this same bug (now moot) via a single
# destructuring assignment instead of three separate statements.
$script := $(convert $file $("/tmp/source_three_statements_repro_{}".format $($random.integer 1000000000)))
cmd rm "-f" $script
$bin.from "echo a\necho b\necho c\n" | $bin.to $script
source $script
cmd rm "-f" $script
